  "articleBody": "If you’ve installed a wheelchair accessible vanity and it’s still not quite hitting the mark, you're not alone. There are a few key areas where installations often fall short, preventing true ease of use. A major issue can be the counter or rim height – if it’s above the **34-inch maximum**, a wheelchair user won't be able to comfortably roll up and use the sink. You also need sufficient clear floor space, typically around **30 by 48 inches**, to allow for proper approach and turning. Without this, the vanity is just a barrier.\n\nAnother critical factor is knee and toe clearance. The space under the sink should ideally be about **27 inches high, 30 inches wide, and 19 inches deep** to accommodate knees and toes. Many standard vanities or even poorly designed accessible ones fail to provide this, leaving plumbing exposed and unprotected, which can be a burn hazard. Also, if the sink bowl is too deep, it can make reaching the faucet difficult, negating the point of the accessible height. Sometimes, people assume a wall-mounted vanity automatically solves all problems, but without checking the specific dimensions and clearances, it might still not be fully functional. \n\nEnsuring all these elements are correctly addressed is vital for a truly functional wheelchair accessible vanity. If you’re struggling with an existing setup or planning a new one, a professional like HH Remodeling LLC can help you verify these critical dimensions and make necessary adjustments to ensure your vanity works perfectly.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
